DESTRUCTIVE ACTIONS AND IRREVERSIBLE WARNINGS — EVERY
APP

\================================================================================



Principle

\- Anything that deletes, overwrites, or cannot be
undone gets explicit confirmation — never a single misclick away.

\- Critical warnings stay inline near the control;
confirmation dialogs use plain consequence language, not jargon.



Requires confirmation (examples — adapt labels to
product)

\- Delete or remove workspace, project, profile, or
stored record

\- Clear vault entry or rotate master password when
data loss is possible

\- Overwrite export or replace file on disk when prior
artifact would be lost

\- Discard unsaved changes when leaving a screen
(Cancel/revert — same family wording everywhere)

\- Remove store account, destination, or irreversible
identifier



Confirmation pattern (same across apps)

\- State what will happen and what cannot be undone in
one or two plain sentences.

\- Destructive confirm uses labeled destructive button
(e.g. “Delete project”) — not generic “OK.”

\- Safe default is Cancel / Keep; primary destructive
action is never the default focused button unless platform requires explicit
tab.

\- No hidden gestures or long-press as the only guard
for destructive work.



Never silently

\- Auto-delete user data on upgrade, tier change, or
trial expiry.

\- Remove files from disk without user-initiated
confirm (except explicit temp/cache cleanup documented in privacy policy).





\================================================================================

VAULT AND MASTER-PASSWORD CONTRACT — EVERY APP

\================================================================================



Principle

\- Secrets and vault behavior are identical in promise
across Nivo78 apps: encrypt at rest, copy-only after save, user holds the keys.



Master password and recovery

\- Master password (or product-equivalent vault key) is
never stored in plain project files or transmitted to Nivo78.

\- When a product uses a master password, it MUST
generate a high-entropy one-time recovery key at first set

  (APA pattern:
grouped unambiguous characters). Show it once with a critical warning: store it
off the device,

  not in the
same place as the master password; Nivo78 never receives it; there is no
email/phone/support reset.

\- The recovery key MUST let the user set a new master
password. It MUST NOT reveal the old password.

 
Password-protected files already sealed with the old password stay tied
to that password unless the product

  also wraps
those files with a data key (document that limit honestly).

\- Using the recovery key MUST issue a new recovery key
and invalidate the old one. Confirm the user saved the

  new key before
the reveal dialog can close.

\- Existing installs that already have a master
password MUST issue a recovery key the next time the user

  enters that
password — never from biometric unlock alone, and never from an unlocked phone
with no secret.

\- Recovery key / recovery mechanism loss is warned
inline — critical, plain language: if both the master

  password and
the recovery key are lost, encrypted secrets and old password-protected files
cannot be recovered.

\- Failed unlock shows honest message — no hint that
reveals secrets; optional retry without leaking vault contents.



Secret entry and display

\- Masked entry for passwords and tokens; save once
through engine coordinator.

\- After save: copy-only access — value not shown on
screen again; replace/remove flows use confirmation when policy requires.

\- Sensitive identifiers (bank, routing, IBAN, payout
IDs) blocked from plain JSON saves where product policy requires vault.



Vault lock and scope

\- Vault lock timeout behavior documented in Help or
security copy when product auto-locks.

\- Unlock scope honest: what requires master password
vs what remains readable plain text in project folders.

\- Clipboard copy of secrets auto-clears after short
timeout when platform supports it; user sees one-line notice.



Order relative to legal gate

\- Document and keep consistent: first-run Terms
acceptance vs master-password setup vs first secret save — same order on all
platforms for the product.





\================================================================================

ERROR, EMPTY, AND LOADING STATES — EVERY APP

\================================================================================



Principle

\- Utility apps fail often (validation, I/O, locked
tiers, vault locked). Every major screen handles non-happy paths without
looking broken.



Error messages

\- Plain language — what went wrong and what to do
next; one primary action when possible.

\- No stack traces, exception class names, or internal
error codes on primary form rows or toasts.

\- Distinguish validation block vs I/O failure vs
policy block (tier locked, vault locked, offline) — different wording, not
generic “Error.”

\- Engine owns message text for business rules; UI
displays engine message without paraphrase that weakens meaning.



Empty states

\- First-use and zero-data screens say what this page
is for and the next visible step — not marketing hero copy.

\- Empty checklist, empty project list, empty search
results: actionable line + visible control (Create, Open, Clear filters).



Loading states

\- Long operations (save, export, load session) show
in-progress guard — prevent double-submit.

\- Loading does not look like success; failed load
fails closed — never show false clean/empty workspace.



Offline and optional network

\- When network is required for user-initiated handoff
(browser, KB, mailto compose): clear offline message — no infinite spinner.

\- Optional online features labeled; offline core never
blocked by optional network failure.

